1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=lawrencetan&id=I136 http://www.nlb.gov.sg/biblioasia/2017/01/04/from-pauper-to-philanthropist-the-tan-tock-seng-story/
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandson Tan Seow Kong:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://eresources.nlb.gov.sg/newspapers/Digitised/Article/maltribune19370515-1.2.87?ST=1&AT=advanced&K=tan%20seow%20kong&KA=tan%20seow%20kong&DF=&DT=09%2F05%2F1940&Display=0&NPT=&L=&CTA=&QT=tan,seow,kong&oref=article
2 CONT
2 CONT Tan Tock Seng was born in Malacca in 1798. He was the third son of an immigrant from Fujian province in China. As a young man full of entrepreneurial drive but no worldly goods, Tan Tock Seng ventured to Singapore to start a small
2 CONC  roadside business. He would buy fruits, vegetables and fowl from the countryside and hawk the fresh food in the City. Hardworking and thrifty, he saved up enough money to open a shop in Boat Quay and proved to be a fine businessm
2 CONC an. It was likely that he spoke English and he made his fortune when he entered into some speculation with an English friend, Mr J.H. Whitehead. When Mr Whitehead died in 1846 at age 36, he was buried at Fort Canning and a tombsto
2 CONC ne was set up bearing this inscription: "... as a token of affection on the part of a Chinese friend, Tan Tock Seng." He owned large tracts of prime land, including 50 acres at the site of the railway station and another plot stre
2 CONC tching from the Padang up to High Street and Tank Road. Other assets were a block of shophouses, an orchard and a nutmeg plantation which he co- owned with a brother. In time, he became an influential Chinese leader and was the fi
2 CONC rst Asian to be made a Justice of the Peace by the Governor. He was skilful at settling feuds among the Chinese. He was known for his generosity and his most famous gesture was the gift of $5,000 to build the Tan Tock Seng Hospita
2 CONC l in 1844. But he also gave widely to other charitable causes, for example, the burial of destitute Chinese, as a proper funeral was important for the Chinese, rich or poor. He was also one of the founders of Singapore's oldest te
2 CONC mple, the Thian Hock Keng at Telok Ayer. This became the centre of worship for the Fujian Chinese. He died in 1850 at age 52. An obituary in the Singapore Free Press described him as one of Singapore's "earliest settlers as well a
2 CONC s most wealthy inhabitants." The paper also praised his contribution as a J.P. : "Much of his time was engrossed in acting as arbitrator in disputes between his countrymen, and many a case which would otherwise have afforded a ric
2 CONC h harvest to the lawyers, was through his intervention and mediation nipped in the bud." He left behind his widow Lee Seo Neo, who owned a large coconut estate in Geylang. Like him, she was unstinting in her support of the hospita
2 CONC l and paid for a female ward. He also left behind three daughters, who were each bequeathed $36,000 in cash. His three sons, including his eldest Tan Kim Ching, inherited his land parcels. It was Kim Ching, a filial son and a dyna
2 CONC mic visionary like his father, who saw to the expansion of the hospital and added personal touches like sending pork and a gift of a few cents to each inmate during Chinese New Year. " Some place of refuge for the number of my unf
2 CONC ortunate countrymen who,at present, while suffering under loathsome diseases crowd the street of the Town and daily obtrude themselves on the public charity having no other means of obtaining relief." Tan Tock Seng October 17,1843
2 CONC  Tan Tock Seng (1798-1850) Original name was Chen Zhousheng (Hanyu Pinyin) Ancestral birthplace was Haicheng district, Fujian province (present-day Longhai district). He was born in Malacca where his family had migrated earlier. T
2 CONC he family was involved in agriculture. He moved from Malacca to Singapore at the age of 21, where he first sold vegetables and poultry for a living. He later opened a business in Boat Quay. He also went into some investment busine
2 CONC ss with an English businessman. It was there that he made his fortune. 1830, he donated Sp $8000 to the building of Hengshanting (a cemetery catering to the Hokkien community). 1830, he became one of the four presidents of the Chi
2 CONC nese temple Thian Hock Keng (still standing in Singapore today), when he donated a sum of $3074.76. 1844, he built the Tan Tock Seng Hospital at Moulmien road (I think it was known then as the Pauper Hospital because it catered so
2 CONC lely to the poor) at the cost of $7000. 1845, he provided burials for 75 beggars who had died destitute on the streets. 1846, he was decorated as a Justice of Peace. He had three sons and three daughters. His sons were Tan Kim Che
2 CONC ng, Tan Swee Lim, and Tan Teck Guan. (Romanisation of the latter two names from Chinese characters). Jalan Tan Tock Seng, near Moulmien Road in Singapore was named after him.  •Note: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tan Tock Seng (b. 1798, Malacca, Malaysia - d. 16 March 1850, Singapore), a Hokkien merchant, landowner, entrepreneur and philanthropist. Tan started as a humble vegetable seller and rose to become one of Singapore's early Chinese
2 CONC  leaders. He was the first Asian to be appointed Justice of the Peace (JP). In 1844, he helped set up a hospital for the poor which now bears his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life Tan left Malacca and came to Singapore in 1819 at the age of 21. The enterprising young man started as a vegetable seller, bringing produce from the countryside to sell in the city. He was hardworking and scrimped on ev
2 CONC ery cent he made. Eventually, with his savings, he opened a shop along the riverside at Boat Quay in 1827.
2 CONT
2 CONT Businessman Through a joint business in land speculation with J. H. Whitehead of Shaw, Whitehead & Co., Tan eventually became a wealthy businessman. His landed properties included 50 acres of land where the railway station at Tanj
2 CONC ong Pagar is located, and the plot of land from the Padang to High Street and Tank Road. He also owned a block of shop houses at Ellenborough Building and a 14-acre fruit plantation opposite the St Andrew's Mission Hospital.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philanthropist Tan contributed generously to charity and became a renowned philanthropist amongst the Chinese. He was known to provide burial costs for the Chinese poor. His most famous gesture was the donation of Spanish $7,000 t
2 CONC o the building of the Chinese Pauper's Hospital (later to be named after him , the Tan Tock Seng Hospital or TTSH) in 1844 at Pearl's Hill. The building was designed by John Turnbull Thomson and it was opened in 1849. He was also 
2 CONC a founder of the Thian Hock Keng Temple at Telok Ayer Street, Singapore's oldest temple, which had been the centre of worship for the Fujian Chinese.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tan Tock Seng was the first Asian to be made a Justice of the Peace by the Governor Butterworth. His role in helping the early Chinese immigrants settle disputes earned him the title "Captain of the Chinese."
2 CONT
2 CONT Tan died at age 52. His grave lies along the grassy slope next to Jubilee Church, 256 Outram Road, Singapore.
