2 CONT Farewell fit for a motor king 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Buffet spread, wine and string ensemble greet mourners at UMW founder's funeral. -TNP  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mon, Feb 14, 2011
2 CONT  The New Paper  
2 CONT
2 CONT String quartet and Hilton Hotel chefs at grand funeral Click on thumbnail to view. Photos: Lianhe Wanbao, BT  
2 CONT
2 CONT By Vivien Chan 
2 CONT
2 CONT IT WAS a grand funeral befitting a patriarch who died at the ripe old age of 93.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ten Mercedes-Benz limousine taxis were hired from 9am to 11pm to ferry mourners to and from the Garden of Remembrance, a Christian columbarium at Old Choa Chu Kang Road.
2 CONT
2 CONT A string quartet was booked to perform. Wreaths lined the perimeter of the funeral parlour.
2 CONT
2 CONT Visitors, seated at white linen-covered tables and chairs, were fed by six chefs, employed to ensure a buffet spread was constantly replenished.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bottles of red wine kept guests' glasses full.
2 CONT
2 CONT More than a thousand relatives and friends streamed into the parlour during the two-day wake, which started on Wednesday, reported Lianhe Wanbao.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of Mr Charlie Chia Cheng Guan spared no effort to make his funeral memorable.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Chia, the former head of United Motor Works (UMW), an automotive and engineering group, died on Monday, leaving behind a wife, two sons, aged 58 and 61, and a daughter, 63.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Chia had helmed the UMW group and turned it into a leading Malaysian conglomerate. Then the family lost it in the 1980s due to financial difficulties.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Mr Timothy Chia Chee Ming, 61, is now chairman of Hup Soon Global, a distributor of industrial equipment as well as automotive and industrial supplies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hup Soon, founded by Mr Timothy Chia in 2005 and listed on the Singapore Exchange in 2007, is the Chinese name for UMW.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wake was a rare display of wealth and status for a family known to keep a low profile.
2 CONT
2 CONT But amid all the finery, one person, arguably the most important in Mr Chia's life, was missing from his final farewell.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, Mrs Janet Chia Yoke Leng, 97, was at home, Mr Timothy Chia told The New Paper.
2 CONT
2 CONT "She is not fully aware of what happened," he said, explaining later that she suffers from dementia.
2 CONT
2 CONT >> Next 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fell at home 
2 CONT
2 CONT He added his father's death was "not a shocker".
2 CONT
2 CONT "He fell down at home on Jan 6, went for surgery and he never really managed to get up because he was in a semi-coma.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Then he passed away peacefully on Feb 7," he said.
2 CONT
2 CONT The elder Mr Chia had lost his balance while trying to get up from the breakfast table and hit his head against a chair.
2 CONT
2 CONT The younger Mr Chia added that his father never had any problems with his balance before that incident.
2 CONT
2 CONT The patriarch was warded at Raffles Hospital, where he later died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr and Mrs Chia celebrated their 70th wedding anniversary just two years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the family has kept out of the media glare, Mr Chia did grant The Business Times an interview in 2009, following his wedding anniversary celebrations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Held at a local hotel in January 2009, the 350 guests whoattended the platinum celebration were given personalised stamps and postcards depicting the couple.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple met in the mid-1930s when Mrs Chia's family made regular trips from Penang to Singapore.
2 CONT
2 CONT They married in January 1939 in Penang.
2 CONT
2 CONT Previous << >> Next 
2 CONT
2 CONT Over the decades, the lovebirds kept their romance alive by travelling the world together, going on driving holidays and maintaining their zest for life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even as they aged, they spent a lot of time together at home, walking in the garden or feeding the fish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Chia spent his entire working life with UMW, which was founded by his father, Mr Chia Yee Soh, in 1917, the same year MrChia was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT He started helping out as a schoolboy, heading to the workshop after school to help his father repair engines.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father's fascination with cars and motorcycles rubbed off on him; Mr Chia got his first car - an Austin Seven -when he was 17.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also a big motorcycle fan and his first bike was made bynone other than Harley-Davidson.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family's helper, 67, who was not named, told Lianhe Wanbao that she joined the Chia family more than 50 years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chias have never been arrogant about their status, she said. They treat the helpers as family members.
2 CONT
2 CONT She added: "They would even share with me delicacies that they buy from their overseas trips."
2 CONT
2 CONT Occasionally, they would take her along on their holidays.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Chia was cremated yesterday afternoon.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was to have celebrated his 94th birthday in two weeks,on Feb 21.
