1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=lawrencetan&id=I152
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee Pek Hoon / Lee Peck Hoon (died 1934) was the Assistant Manager of the Straits Steamship Co. In 1888, Lee Pek Hoon was appointed to act as an agent for Hong Kong, Amoy and Swatow for Bun Hin & Co. line of steam ships (founded b
2 CONC y prominent shipping merchant, Khoo Tiong Poh) and for 10 years managed it from China before moving to Singapore. He also owned a theatre named "Lee Peck Soon Theatre" at 499 North Bridge Road which performed Malay and Indian thea
2 CONC tre performances. He was a shareholder of Soon Keck, Ltd and Nyalas Rubber Estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the Straits Chinese British Association, Lee Pek Hoon has a wide range of interesting throughout his life, ranging from rearing prized poultry (chickens), to an avid orchid cultivator (his orchid's have won prizes) and
2 CONC  finally a race horse owner. He was one of the principal member's of the "Chinese Riding Party", a group of avid horse riders which included Dr Lim Boon Keng, Tan Boo Liat, Tan Chew Kim, Tan Hup Leong and Phua Choon Hiang. . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Contributions Halifax Relief Fund (1918) "Our Day" (1917) Tientsin Relief Fund (1918) China Famine Fund (1920), Swatow Relief Fund (1922) Singapore Chinese Girls School New Building Fund (1925)
2 CONT
2 CONT Death Lee Pek Hoon died in December 4, 1934 and is buried in Hill 3 Division D, plot 1002. His age of death listed in burial registrar is 68 years. Listed on his tomb are the name of 2 sons (however my chalking was not clear) and 
2 CONC 3 daughters (Mary Lee, Polly Lee and Iris Lee). Will try to visit again to confirm the names. His residence in his early years was at Syed Ali Road and later on 40 Newton Road.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://mymindisrojak.blogspot.my/2012/10/lee-pek-hoon-bukit-brown.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee Pek Hoon / Lee Peck Hoon (died 1934) was born in Singapore and was educated in St Joseph's Institution in both English and Chinese. He speaks several dialects as well as some Japanese. He first joined Messrs. Harris, Goodwin a
2 CONC nd Co before taking up in 1888, an appointment to act as an agent for Hong Kong, Amoy and Swatow for Llyods' Khw Tiong Po Bun Hin & Co. line of steam ships (founded by prominent shipping merchant, Khoo Tiong Poh) and for 10 years 
2 CONC managed it from China before moving back to Singapore in 1898, becoming the Assistant Manager of the Straits Steamship Co. Lee Pek Hoon also owned a theatre named "Lee Peck Soon Theatre" at 499 North Bridge Road which performed Ma
2 CONC lay and Indian theatre performances. He was a shareholder of Soon Keck, Ltd and Nyalas Rubber Estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee Pek Hoon 
2 CONT
2 CONT  A member of the Straits Chinese British Association, Lee Pek Hoon has a wide range of interesting throughout his life, ranging from rearing prized poultry (chickens), to an avid orchid cultivator (his orchid's have won prizes) an
2 CONC d  finally a race horse owner. He was one of the principal member's of the "Chinese Riding Party", a group of avid horse riders which included Dr Lim Boon Keng, Tan Boo Liat, Tan Chew Kim, Tan Hup Leong and Phua Choon Hiang. He ow
2 CONC ned many racehorses under the name of P.H. Lee. He won the Penang Derby with his horse Pawnbroker. Lee Pek Hoon married a daughter of Mr Tan Kung Hoe.
2 CONT
2 CONT  His father is also a merchant by the name of Lee Cheng Tee. Another interesting fact is that Lee Pek Hoon's mother is Tan Hay Neo, one of Tan Tock Seng's daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee Pek Hoon  
2 CONT
2 CONT Recent photo of Lee Pek Hoon to capture names cleaerer 
2 CONT
2 CONT Contributions
2 CONT  Halifax Relief Fund (1918) "Our Day" (1917) Tientsin Relief Fund (1918)  China Famine Fund (1920), Swatow Relief Fund (1922) Singapore Chinese Girls School New Building Fund (1925)
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT  Lee Pek Hoon passed away on December 4, 1934 and is buried in Hill 3 Division D, plot 1002. His age of death listed in burial registrar is 68 years. Listed on his tomb are the name of 2 sons (however my chalking was not clear) an
2 CONC d 3 daughters (Mary Lee, Polly Lee and Iris Lee). Will try to visit again to confirm the names. His residence in his early years was at Syed Ali Road and later on 40 Newton Road.
2 CONT
2 CONT  After re-chalking, you can see clearer:
2 CONT  In Loving Memory of Lee Pek Hoon,
2 CONT  born October 5th, 1868,  died December 4th 1934, age 66 years.
2 CONT  Sons: John Lee, Henry Lee
2 CONT  Daughters: Mary Lee, Polly Lee, Iris Lee
2 CONT  Son-in-law: Oh Jitt Siang
2 CONT  Daughter-in-law: Teo Ghek Khee
2 CONT  Grandson: Hugh Lee, Harry Lee, Harold Lee, Hubert Lee
2 CONT                    Oh Joo Lian, Oh Cheng Lian, Oh Boon Lian
2 CONT  Grand-daughter: Ivy Oh, Mavis Oh, Vivien Oh
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Father: Lee Cheng Tee
2 CONT  Lee Cheng Tee (once partner of Cheng-tee, Watt-Seng & Co, shipowners) was born in Malacca in 1833 and in his early years traded with Labuan and Brunei. Together with his partner, Wee Watt Seng, their company traded in Java ports 
2 CONC for many years. He also owned a steamship called Telegraph which sailed to Malacca and Penang. He partnered with Mr. Tan Seng Poh and owned a gunpowder magazine at Tanah Merah Kechil. Retiring at an early age from business, Lee Ch
2 CONC eng Tee built a large house at Victoria Street until it was acquired by Mr. A. Frankel  when he expanded his furniture business.
2 CONT
2 CONT  A tall and well built man, Mr. Lee Cheng Tee passed away at the age of 68 (he embraced Christianity in his twilight years) on March 12, 1901 and is buried in the Christian Cemetery in Bukit Timah Road. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mother: Tan Hay Neo
2 CONT  Tan Hay Neo (daughter of Tan Tock Seng and Lee See Neo and younger sister of Tan Kim Cheng). Madam Tan Hay Neo passed away in August 1892 in her residence in Victoria Street and is buried in Bukit Timah Road, 5th mile.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Brothers: Lee Pek Swee and Lee Pek Hock, O.B.E.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother: Lee Pek Hock 
2 CONT  Lee Pek Hock is married to Yeo Kim Neo (died January 26, 1927) and have 2 sons (Lee Boon Chuan and Lee Kim Poh) and 1 daughter (Lee Swe Har who married Chua Keh Hai) and resides in No 360 Orchard Road. A businessman, Lee Pek Hock
2 CONC 's O.B.E ( Order of the British Empire) given to him on June 1923 was annulled and cancelled in 1929 after he faced civil conviction for his bankruptcies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lee Pek Hock
2 CONT  (source: NewspaperSG) 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Son: Johnnie Lee
2 CONT  Lee Boon Kong, Johnnie married Miss Teo Geok Kee, only daughter of Mr and Mrs Teo Thiam Swee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter: Mary Lee
2 CONT  In November 1912, Miss. Mary Lee was engaged to Tjong Coon Whie, son of Tjong Eng Hean, Mayor of Bali and a well known millionaire in Deli. (Medan was also called Deli in the past). This makes me wonder whether there is any relat
2 CONC ion to Tjong A Fie / Tjong Yiauw Hian or his brother Tjong Yong Hian of Medan. Tjong A Fie was the Kapitan Cina of Medan in 1911 after his brother, Tjong Yong Hian passed away.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In another article dated 1913 (one year after Mary's engagement) it mentions a Mrs Tjong Coon Whie (i am assuming Mary Lee), but what is more interesting is that it mentions Mr Tjong Coon Whie father as deceased and with a differ
2 CONC ent spelling variant (Tjong Yoong Hean) and was the former Mayor of Deli which makes me more suspicious about the Medan connection.
2 CONT
2 CONT source: NewspaperSG 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stories from No 40 Newton Road  Servants rout robbers (1928)
2 CONT  In 1928, there was an attempted robbery by 4 chinese armed with knives at Lee Pek Hoon's house at 40 Newton Road, but thanks to bravery of his 2 Javanese gardener, his chinese gardener and a driver, the attempted daylight robbery
2 CONC  at 11:30 am was routed.  A police reward of $50 and letter of commendation was given to 3 of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Weightlifting Competition (1928)
2 CONT  Lee Pek Hoon allowed his residence to be used as a venue for a weightlifting competition. His son, Johnnie Lee was the organiser.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rice Distribution Depot (1920)
2 CONT  Lee Pek Hoon's house was one of the 42 depots used for rice distribution in 1920, at the time when the British Crown Colony was trying to control prices of rice, prevent hoarding and fair distribution through a rice rationing exe
2 CONC rcise via household coupons. Lee Pek Hoon was one of the many notable chinese pioneers (others include Lim Nee Soon, Khoo Kay Hian, Ong Boon Tat, See Tiong Wah, Tan Boo Liat) offered their premises for free to be such depots.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lost Dog
2 CONT
2 CONT Lost of "Sweep", Lee Pek Hoon's retriever dog
2 CONT  (source: NewspaperSG) 
