1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Tan Chay Yan J.P.  (b. December 1871, Malacca–d. 6 March 1916, Malacca), also known as Tan Chay An or Chen Qixian, was the first rubber planter in Malaya. He pioneered an industry that transformed the region’s fort
2 CONC unes, and used his wealth to support causes such as education.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://eresources.nlb.gov.sg/infopedia/articles/SIP_1628_2009-12-31.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://reader.library.cornell.edu/docviewer/digital?id=sea:233#page/848/mode/1up
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=lawrencetan&id=I148
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried with his wife CHUA Wan Neo  •Note: Born in Malacca in 1870 and was 26 years old when his father, TTG passed away. He died of beriberi in March 1916 at a relatively young age. Inherited vast landed properties in Malacca and 
2 CONC Singapore. Married Madam Chua who gave birth to a son in 1909, Hoon Siang Robert and a daughter. Was a JP, a Municipal Councillor and a Trustee of the Cheng Hoon Teng Temple. Founded the Pay Fong Chinese School. Noted in Malayan h
2 CONC istory as the first man to plant rubber on a commercial (estate) basis. He was introduced to rubber planting by Ridley and started a 22-acre rubber estate at Bukit Lintang. In a horticultural show in 1896, he won a trophy for bein
2 CONC g the first man to produce a rubber sheet. Later together with his friends, they started the Bukit Asahan Rubber Estate (near Mount Orphir), a 3,000 acre venture. Later the estate was sold to a big London company called the Malacc
2 CONC a Rubber Plantation Ltd. He spent $15,000 to erect an annexe building to the Singapore Medical School in Outram in memory of of his father. The building called the Tan Teck Guan Building -- still stands today -- provided space for
2 CONC  a big lecture room, a reading room and a museum. The TTG Building was officially opened by the then Acting Governor of the Straits Settlements, the Hon. Mr E L Brockman in 1911.  •Change Date: 3 OCT 2011 at 16:11:56
2 CONT
