2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Lim Peng Siang was born in Arnoy in the Fukien province of China in 1872. After receiving his education in Chinese, he came to Singapore when he was still very young. He acquired most of his English education from private tuit
2 CONC ion, though to complete same he was for a year a student at the St. Joseph's Institution. He afterwards joined the now defunct firm of Wee Bin & Co., which was then under the management of his father, the late Mr. Lim Ho Puah. His
2 CONC  business acumen was of a high order, and the firm of Ho Hong Co., which he first started in 1904 with a modest capital, soon developed into a big concern. Unlike most Chinese, who either send their accumulated wealth to China or 
2 CONC have it invested in mortgages or real estate, Mr. Lim Peng Siang utilised practically every cent of his money in various industrial developments, thereby giving employment to several thousand people. Among the other Ho Hong concer
2 CONC ns which owe their existence to Mr. Lim Peng Siang are The Ho Hong Steamship Co. Ltd., The Ho Hong Oil Mills Ltd., The Ho Hong Parboiled Rice Mill, The Ho Hong Bank Ltd., and the Ho Hong Portland Cement Works Ltd. He had other sch
2 CONC emes and the necessary machinery ready for a bucket-making factory and for the reclamation and development of several big pieces of swampy land in a big industrial area in the immediate neighbourhood of Singapore Town. It will be 
2 CONC seen therefore how great a benefactor Mr. Lim Peng Siang has been to Singapore. It is hardly necessary to mention here how much a country depends on industry and shipping for its wealth and importance. It can be clearly seen to wh
2 CONC at extent Mr. Lim Peng Siang has contributed to both these factors. From time to time severe competition with other steamship lines reduced deck-passage rates to a ridiculously low figure and it also meant heavy loss to the firm: 
2 CONC but this proved a boon to thousands of the labouring classes who were enabled to leave their homes in China and come to the Straits Settlements and the Netherlands East Indies to supply the labour market. Like his father, Mr. Lim 
2 CONC Peng Siang was a naturalised British subject. He had been so since 1902. He was one of the leading men among the Chinese merchants of Singapore and was greatly respected by the community. He took an active interest in the formatio
2 CONC n of the Singapore Chinese Chamber of Commerce and has been one of its Presidents. He was a member of the Chinese Advisory Board, on which he has served for many years as one of the representatives of the Hokien (Fukien) community
2 CONC , and was a J .P .He was a director of a number of public companies, including the Central Engine Works Ltd. and the Central Motors Ltd. In hios later years he was of a retiring disposition in so far as public activities were conc
2 CONC erned, and though offered a seat on the Legislative Council on several occasions he hhad been obliged to decline it, having to give his whole attention to the numerous industries which he had built up. Besides, the years of hard w
2 CONC ork and incessant toil had begun to tell rather heavily on his health. During the Great War he proved his patriotism by working hard in helping to raise money for the various funds, besides himself liberally contributing to such f
2 CONC unds. He was never known to refuse help to a deserving cause, and innumerable were the charities to which he liberally contributed. He set an example worthy of being followed by the rising members of the Chinese community.  •Chang
2 CONC e Date: 15 OCT 2012 at 17:13:50
2 CONT
