1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://wc.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=lawrencetan&id=I6765
2 CONT
2 CONT ID: I6765  Name: YEO Hong Tye  Sex: M 1  Note:  Yeo Hong Tye is the son of one of the five brothers Yeo Hooding, Yeo Chi Gwen, Yeo Hoot King, Yeo Hoot Seng and Yeo Hoot Hin who carried on business,together in co-partnership as lea
2 CONC ding Chinese Merchantile house, operating in Boat Quay area in 1840s under the firm or chop of "Kong Chung" of "Kong Cheang". The firm continued until 1865, when it was dissolved, owing to the death of several of its partners.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1827, Yeo Hooding already appeared in public records as a landowner, together with Tan Tock Seng, Yeo Ching Hai and Tan Oo Long, who became prominent citizens in the later history of Singapore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the five brothers, only one, Yeo Hoot Seng, was mentioned in the public records. He was on the committee of the Tan Tock Seng Hospital from 1850 - 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about YEO Hong Tye himself, except for the fact that was married to Tan Geok Hup, the daughter of Tan Kim Seng, sister of Tan Beng Swee, Tan Beng Gwee and Tan Beng Gam and Tan Geok Ee.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 5 Yeo brothers owned a large pocket of land in Upper Telok Blangah, now known in part as Pasir Panjang. This estate was commonly known as the Hooding Estate. They had bought this land in 1851 from the British East India Compan
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT The descendents of Yeo Hooding &/or his brothers continued to live on part of the estate until it was almost completely acquired by the Singapore Government in 1960's. The last occupants on the Hooding estate were Yeo Kiat Seng (s
2 CONC on of Yeo Cheow Bock, grandson of Yeo Hong Thye), his wife, Lim Poh Neo and their three children, George Yeo, Irene Yeo and Robert Yeo Peck Chye. On the property now stands a condominium, a petrol station and Yeo's Building. Yeo's
2 CONC  Building is what remains of the family estate - built from the funds paid by the government for the acquisition of part of 115 acres of land endowed by Yeo Hong Tye and his wife, Tan Geok Hup, for the burial of all Yeos of Hokkie
2 CONC n descent, by an indenture dated 8th Nov 1882. This indenture was lodged by the executrix of of estate of Yeo Hong Tye - Tan Ching Neo (believed to be a relative and possibly the daughter of Tan Tock Seng J.P.). The land was known
2 CONC  as Heap Guan Sun and was believed to have span more than 115 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT The said land (save for 12+ acres reserved for the ancestral home) was vested in Tan Geop Hup by Yeo Hong Tye by a deed dated 16th September 1882. In the deed, Tan Geop Hup agreed to settle the said land into the trust of Yeo Hong
2 CONC  Tye and Tan Jiak Kim to grant the land as a burial ground for all persons of "the Hokkien tribe bearing the surname or clan name Yeo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Hoot Eng YEO (aka YEO Hooding) b: 1796
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 TAN Geok Hup b: AFT 1829 in Singapore
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT  YEO Bee Hean
2 CONT  YEO Bee Lye
2 CONT  YEO Bee Luan
